  "verdict": "Unproven",
  "steelman": "A comprehensive federal systematic review by the National Toxicology Program found an association between higher fluoride exposures—exceeding 1.5 milligrams per liter—and lower IQ scores in children, which has fueled widespread concern over potential neurodevelopmental risks from community water supplies.",
  "finding": "The evidence is mixed. A systematic review by the National Toxicology Program concluded with moderate confidence that drinking water containing more than 1.5 milligrams of fluoride per liter is associated with lower IQ in children. However, the review found insufficient data to determine whether the 0.7 mg/L concentration recommended for U.S. community water supplies has a negative effect on children's IQ. None of the epidemiological studies in the federal meta-analysis were conducted in the United States, and researchers noted that an association does not prove cause and effect.",
  "reasoning_flags": [
    "Correlation, not cause"
  ],
  "newest_data": "National Toxicology Program Monograph 08 on Fluoride Exposure and Neurodevelopment and Cognition (released August 2024), its associated meta-analysis (January 2025), and the NTP assessment report page (last updated September 18, 2026). A search across federal health and environmental agencies as of October 2026 confirmed no newer systematic review or official assessment on fluoride and child neurocognition has been published.",
